tter to play the *higher* of your low cards first to avoid getting stuck later with unplayable stragglers. Also, ♣9 is less likely to be matched or continued by the opponent than lower cards, possibly leading to a faster turnover.\n\nStill, the difference in value is minimal, and preserving the slightly higher card (♣9) might be better for later if we get another chance. Therefore, starting with the lowest playable card (♦4) maintains flexibility and keeps a marginally stronger card in reserve.\n\nReason: Since this is a free play, I must play. Between the two singles, ♦4 is the lowest and safest lead — it avoids prematurely escalating while fulfilling the requirement to play. It preserves ♣9 as a future option, which could be useful if a similar low-single situation arises again. Given that the teammate has 5 cards and likely wants control, playing small allows them more flexibility to win with a modest card if possible.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦4"]}'}
